---
/**
 * Findings: the recon port rail, as a semantic definition list.
 *
 * Wraps a run of <Finding port="..."> children in a <dl class="findings"> whose two columns are
 * COMPUTED by a grid. Nothing here declares how wide the port column is: `max-content` measures the
 * widest chip actually present and the second track takes the rest. That is the whole point of the
 * component. The rail it replaced declared the relationship as three coupled literals (li
 * padding-left / text-indent 5.6em against a chip min-width 4.9em plus margin-right 0.7em, one
 * intended identity since 4.9 + 0.7 = 5.6) which resolved in two different font-size contexts, the
 * li's 18px and the chip's 14px, and were therefore never equal. See CORE_SPEC section 8.
 *
 * <Finding> deliberately renders TWO sibling elements rather than a wrapper, so every dt and dd is a
 * direct child of this dl and participates in this grid. A wrapper per row would need subgrid, and
 * subgrid on a row containing inline <code> blockifies that code onto its own track.
 */
---

<dl class="findings"><slot /></dl>

<style>
  /* SCOPING NOTE: dt and dd are rendered by Finding.astro, a different component, so they carry a
     different Astro scope id and a plain `.findings dt` would compile to a selector that can never
     match them. :global() on the CHILD, with the scoped .findings as its ancestor, is the contained
     form: it is bounded by this dl and never escapes it.

     This block styles LAYOUT ONLY. The chip's colour, background, border, radius, font-family and
     font-size stay in the components layer of the theme pass, deliberately untouched here: an Astro
     scoped style is unlayered, so anything declared here would silently outrank the theme pass at any
     specificity, which is exactly the failure mode the layer contract exists to prevent. */
  .findings {
    display: grid;
    /* The port column measures itself. No literal, no floor, no padding of short tokens. */
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    /* Chip and description sit on one shared baseline, as they did in inline flow. Without this,
       grid's default `normal` alignment stretches the chip to the row height and tears its box open. */
    align-items: baseline;
    /* Derived from the chip, not picked: the chip's own inline padding is 0.45em at its pinned 14px,
       which is 6.3px a side. A gutter has to exceed the chip's internal padding or the chip reads as
       indented text rather than as a bounded object. Two times 6.3 is 12.6px, rounded onto the rem
       grid at 0.8rem (12.8px). rem, not em: this is component geometry, not a word space. */
    column-gap: 0.8rem;
    /* Reproduces the old rail's rhythm exactly. The li margins were 0.26rem and COLLAPSED to 0.26rem
       between rows (measured, not assumed); grid does not collapse margins, so the same number is
       declared once as a gap. A translation, not a change. */
    row-gap: 0.26rem;
    /* Reproduces the old ul margins so the callout's outer geometry does not move. */
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.2rem;
  }

  /* Chips keep their natural widths and meet the column edge on the RIGHT, so the gutter is exactly
     column-gap on every row regardless of token length. This is what makes the description column
     start at one x with no arithmetic anywhere. */
  .findings :global(dt) {
    justify-self: end;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Starlight ships dd { margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 16px } and dl { margin-top: 16px } (measured).
     The dl margin is replaced above; these two are zeroed so the grid alone positions the row. */
  .findings :global(dd) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Authoring a Finding in FLOW position (tags on their own lines) makes MDX wrap the description in
     a <p>; authoring it inline, which is what all fifteen rails do, does not. Measured both ways on a
     scratch page. This matches nothing today and exists so a later multi-line description cannot
     introduce an 18px paragraph margin inside a grid row. */
  .findings :global(dd > p) {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
